<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Razor">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <IsPackable>true</IsPackable>
    <RootNamespace>Rask.External</RootNamespace>

    <!--
      client/*.ts are the adapters, shipped as SOURCE for the consumer's bundler to compile. They are
      not scoped assets and there is no component named 'react' or 'adapter' for them to pair with, so
      the scoped-TS convention (#871) would report each of them as an orphan (RASK017).

      A property rather than an <_RaskScopedTs Remove="…"/>, because this project body is evaluated
      before Directory.Build.targets imports Rask.Core.targets, so there is no item list here yet to
      remove anything from — the same reason Rask.Spa.Hosting sets it for its own vendored client.
    -->
    <RaskScopedTsAutoInclude>false</RaskScopedTsAutoInclude>

    <PackageId>Rask.External</PackageId>
    <Title>Rask — Foreign Components</Title>
    <Description>Lets a .tsx, .jsx or Lit component be an ordinary Rask component. Mark a component [Island], drop the front-end file beside it, and its props are declared in C# — the generator emits the TypeScript interface the file imports, so renaming a C# property stops the front end compiling. The live diff treats the island's subtree as opaque, because those nodes belong to its own renderer.</Description>
    <PackageTags>web component framework wasm net10 react lit islands typescript interop</PackageTags>

    <!-- Razor SDK for its static-web-asset pipeline only (no .razor files), same as Rask.Bootstrap:
         it is what puts wwwroot/rask-external.js on a URL both hosts resolve identically, in-repo and
         from the packed NuGet. Set explicitly so those two agree. -->
    <StaticWebAssetBasePath>_content/Rask.External</StaticWebAssetBasePath>

    <!-- Provide build/Rask.External.props ourselves instead of letting the Static Web Assets SDK
         auto-generate it, so the same file can carry the RaskExternal* properties the targets stand on
         AND import the static-web-asset wiring. The SDK still emits
         build/Microsoft.AspNetCore.StaticWebAssets*.props (which our file imports) plus the
         buildMultiTargeting/buildTransitive shims that import our build/ file.

         Without this, BOTH files claim build/Rask.External.props: the SDK's is packed first and ours
         is dropped with NU5118, so the shipped package carries a 163-byte import shim in place of the
         real props. In-repo everything still works, because Directory.Build.props imports the real
         file straight off disk — so no build, test or E2E gate can see it. Only `dotnet pack` can.
         Same reasoning as Rask.Bootstrap, which hit this first. -->
    <StaticWebAssetsDisableProjectBuildPropsFileGeneration>true</StaticWebAssetsDisableProjectBuildPropsFileGeneration>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- PrivateAssets="all" — Rask.Core and Rask.Html are IsPackable=false, so without it the nuspec
         would list dependencies on packages that exist on no feed and every restore fails NU1101.
         Consumers get both from whichever host package they already reference. -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Rask.Core\Rask.Core.csproj" PrivateAssets="all"/>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Rask.Html\Rask.Html.csproj" PrivateAssets="all"/>
    <!--
      The generator has to RUN here, not merely be available to consumers. A referenced library's
      components cannot be reached from a chain unless that library publishes its own
      RaskEntries{Assembly} class — which is what this emits. Without it ExternalSlot compiles, ships,
      and is simply unreachable at every call site, with nothing reporting why.

      It also publishes the required-property assembly attributes RASK038 reads across an assembly
      boundary: metadata cannot distinguish `string Named` from `string Named = ""`, so a consumer
      cannot re-derive which chain steps are mandatory.
    -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Rask.Generators\Rask.Generators.csproj"
                      OutputItemType="Analyzer"
                      ReferenceOutputAssembly="false"/>
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      Build-order-only edge to the MSBuild task project, which drops Rask.External.Tasks.dll into
      build/ here (CopyTaskAssemblyToRaskExternalBuild) for <UsingTask> to load and for packing. The
      DLL is a generated artifact, gitignored rather than committed, so this reference is what
      guarantees it exists before this project builds or packs. ReferenceOutputAssembly="false" keeps
      it purely an ordering edge — no compile reference, no nupkg dependency, and no cross-TFM
      (net10.0 vs netstandard2.0) compatibility check.
    -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Rask.External.Tasks\Rask.External.Tasks.csproj"
                      ReferenceOutputAssembly="false"
                      PrivateAssets="all"/>
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      The task assembly is named EXPLICITLY and excluded from the glob beside it, because a glob is
      expanded at project EVALUATION — before any target runs, including the build of the
      ProjectReference that produces this DLL. On a tree where it is not already on disk the glob
      matches nothing and `dotnet pack` SUCCEEDS, shipping a package whose <UsingTask> points at a
      file that is not in it; the consumer fails with MSB4062, and a second pack on the same machine
      looks fine because the first build left the DLL behind (#852).

      A literal Include needs the file only when the item is copied, which is after the reference has
      built — so it is collected, and if it is ever genuinely missing the pack FAILS instead of
      quietly shipping without it.
    -->
    <None Include="build\**" Exclude="build\Rask.External.Tasks.dll" Pack="true" PackagePath="build\"/>
    <None Include="build\Rask.External.Tasks.dll" Pack="true" PackagePath="build\"/>
    <!-- The adapters, shipped as source the way Rask.Spa.Hosting ships its client. Vendored into obj/
         on every build, so upgrading this package upgrades both halves together. -->
    <None Include="client\**" Pack="true" PackagePath="client\"/>
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Rask.External.Tests"/>
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
